"by Offline Base\n·\nTry open\nSelf Chat\nTry any open model\nGLM 5.3 is replying\nShare\nExport\nNew chat\n\nArchitektur/Datenkontrakt, Antwort terse (Verdikt + Begruendung). Wire = fix 26xf64, kein Umbau; epoch = TDB-Sekunden seit J2000, 0.0 = J2000.0 (echter Wert). Ein statisches Gravitationsmodell (gfc) hat gar keine Epoche, ein zeitvariables (gfct) hat eine. Wir verwerfen das Presence-Bit und wollen einen Modellart-Unterscheider als Wahrheitsquelle. Gemessene Haus-Praezedenz: Quellen tragen schon ein source-level Option-Epochenfeld (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch), der Frame traegt Koerper/Modus, ICGEM taggt selbst gfc/gfct pro Datensatz. Frage: Wo muss der Unterscheider leben — (a) pro Record in einem 26xf64-Slot, (b) source-/frame-level, (c) beides — und was enummeriert er genau (statisch|zeitvariabel vs. epoche-anwendbar|nicht)? Wie bleibt ein roher Wire-Leser ohne Quellwissen ehrlich? Nenne den staerksten Riss und die Kosten jeder Option.\n\nGLM 5.3\nAnswered by\nZ.ai\nThinking…\nThe user is asking a technical architecture question in German about a data contract design. They want a terse answer: verdict + reasoning. Let me parse the question carefully.\n\nContext:\n- Wire format = fixed 26×f64 (26 float64 slots), no restructuring allowed (\"kein Umbau\")\n- epoch = TDB seconds since J2000, 0.0 = J2000.0 (a real value, not a sentinel)\n- Static gravity model (gfc) has no epoch at all; time-variable model (gfct) has one\n- They're dropping a presence-bit approach and want a \"model type discriminator\" as source of truth\n- Measured house precedent: sources already carry a source-level optional epoch field (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch), the frame carries body/mode, ICGEM itself tags gfc/gfct per dataset\n\nQuestions:\n1. Where must the discriminator live — (a) per-record in one of the 26 f64 slots, (b) source-/frame-level, (c) both — and what does it enumerate exactly (static|time-variable vs. epoch-applicable|not)?\n2. How does a raw wire reader without source knowledge stay honest?\n3. Name the strongest tear/crack (\"Riss\") and the costs of each option.\n\nLet me think about this like a serious data-contract architect.\n\nKey facts:\n- ICGEM format: gfc = static gravity field coefficients, gfct = time-variable (has epoch). The gfc/gfct distinction is a property of the *dataset/model*, not of an individual record. Every record in a gfc file is static; every record in a gfct file belongs to a specific epoch. So the discriminator is inherently dataset-scoped (constant per file/model), not record-scoped.\n- The wire format is fixed 26×f64. Encoding an enum into an f64 slot is possible (0.0 = static, 1.0 = time-variable) but it's a hack: it puts a categorical value into a numeric slot, it's a per-record repetition of a constant, and it consumes a slot. Also 0.0 is a *valid epoch value* (J2000.0), so if you tried to overload the epoch slot with a sentinel, you'd collide — but they explicitly said epoch 0.0 is a real value, which is exactly why the presence-bit / sentinel approach is being dropped. So the lesson: no sentinel overloading in numeric slots.\n- House precedent: source-level already has optional epoch fields (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch); frame carries body/mode; ICGEM tags gfc/gfct per dataset. So the metadata layers already exist and already carry the discriminating information — it's just not unified into one field.\n\nThe core question: where does the discriminator live?\n\nAnalysis:\n\n(a) Per-record in a 26×f64 slot:\n- Pros: raw wire reader sees it without any context; self-describing record.\n- Cons: costs a slot; repeats a dataset-constant per record (denormalization); encoding an enum in f64 is type abuse; and it reintroduces the sentinel problem — what value is \"static\"? 0.0 collides with J2000.0 real epoch... well, actually if it's a dedicated slot, 0.0=static, 1.0=tv doesn't collide with epoch. But it's still ugly. More importantly: the gfc/gfct distinction does NOT vary per record. Putting a constant in every record is a denormalized copy of metadata, which can now disagree with the source-level truth → two sources of truth, drift.\n- Also: the slot is only 1 bit of information per record but costs 8 bytes per record.\n\n(b) Source-/frame-level:\n- Pros: matches the ontological truth — static-vs-time-variable is a property of the model (dataset), like body/mode which already lives on the frame. ICGEM already tags gfc/gfct per dataset, so ingestion can map it 1:1. No wire cost. No sentinel collisions. One authoritative location.\n- Cons: a raw wire reader without source/frame context cannot tell whether the epoch slot (wherever it lives — actually wait, where does epoch live on the wire?).\n\nHmm, let me think about where epoch lives. The wire is 26 f64 fixed. The epoch = TDB seconds since J2000. Presumably one of the 26 slots is the epoch (or epoch lives in the source-level optional field catalog_epoch/ref_epoch?). The question says: \"Quellen tragen schon ein source-level Option-Epochenfeld (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch)\". So epoch is at source level, optional. And they dropped the presence-bit. So presumably: when the model is static, the epoch field is absent; when time-variable, it's present. But they don't want presence = truth because... the presence-bit approach was rejected (\"Wir verwerfen das Presence-Bit und wollen einen Modellart-Unterscheider als Wahrheitsquelle\"). Why? Probably because an optional field's presence is a weak, ambiguous signal — presence of catalog_epoch could mean \"we happen to know the catalog epoch\" rather than \"the model is time-variable\". Presence conflates \"field known\" with \"model type\". That's the classic optional-field-as-discriminator anti-pattern. Also 0.0-epoch is real, so null-vs-0.0 semantics can get muddled.\n\nSo the decision: the discriminator is a model-type enum. What does it enumerate?\n\nOption 1: static | time-variable (gfc | gfct) — mirrors ICGEM.\nOption 2: epoch-applicable | epoch-not-applicable — mirrors what the consumer actually needs to know: \"may I use the epoch field?\"\n\nThe subtlety: these two are not identical. \"Epoch-applicable\" is a statement about the schema semantics (does the epoch field carry meaning for this record/dataset), while \"static|time-variable\" is a statement about physics/model kind. In practice for ICGEM they coincide 1:1 today (gfc → no epoch, gfct → epoch). But they can diverge: e.g., a static model could still have a reference epoch (when the static coefficients were estimated — gfc files in ICGEM can actually have... hmm, actually gfc has no epoch in ICGEM, but a static model derived at a reference time might legitimately carry a \"reference epoch of estimation\" which is metadata, not applicability). If the enum says \"static\", the reader infers \"epoch not applicable\" — an inference. If the enum says \"epoch-applicable|not\", it's directly the wire-level contract statement, and static/tv becomes derivable upstream.\n\nThe architecturally cleaner answer: the discriminator should enumerate what the *consumer of that layer* needs. At the source/frame level (metadata layer), enumerate the domain concept: model_kind = static | time_variable (gfc|gfct mapping). That's the truth about the world. The epoch-applicability is a *derived* fact: static → epoch must be absent/ignored; time_variable → epoch required. So you don't enumerate epoch-applicability as a separate enum; you state it as an invariant/validation rule in the contract: \"model_kind=static ⇒ epoch absent; model_kind=time_variable ⇒ epoch present (may be 0.0 = J2000, a real value)\".\n\nSo: (b) source/frame level — specifically, where? Frame carries body/mode; source carries optional epoch fields. The model kind is a property of the *dataset/model*, and ICGEM tags it per dataset. House precedent: frame = physical/contextual identity (body, mode), source = provenance-ish fields (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch). Is model_kind frame-level or source-level? The gfc/gfct tag comes from the ICGEM *file*, i.e., from the source. Each source (dataset) is uniformly gfc or gfct. But different sources in the same frame could be... can a frame mix static and time-variable sources? Possibly — e.g., a frame for Earth gravity could reference a static model and a time-variable one. Then the discriminator must be per-source, not per-frame, because frame-level would force homogeneity. Actually, hmm — the question says \"der Frame trägt Körper/Modus\" (frame carries body/mode). Body/mode is also uniform per frame. But model kind can differ per source within a frame (a static background field plus time-variable additions is a very real scenario in geodesy — mean field + trend/annual terms; ICGEM gfct is often accompanied by a gfc mean). So the discriminator belongs at **source level** (dataset level), next to the existing epoch fields — one level below frame, one above record.\n\nBut wait — the strongest argument for (c) both: the raw wire reader. \"Wie bleibt ein roher Wire-Leser ohne Quellwissen ehrlich?\" A raw wire reader (only sees 26 f64) without source knowledge: if the discriminator is only at source level, the reader *cannot* know whether slot semantics include a meaningful epoch. How does it stay honest? Answer: it can't interpret, but it can stay honest by *not guessing*: treat all 26 as opaque f64, or the contract must guarantee that record-level layout is identical regardless of model kind (fixed layout = the whole point of fix 26xf64). Actually — with a fixed 26-slot layout, does the epoch live in a slot or at source level? The question says sources carry source-level optional epoch fields. So maybe the wire record doesn't carry the epoch at all — epoch is metadata! Then the raw wire reader never sees an epoch, and honesty is trivial: the 26 slots mean the same thing for static and tv models; the epoch (source-level) applies to the whole batch. Hmm, but then what would a per-record discriminator even discriminate? If the record layout is identical for gfc and gfct, a per-record discriminator slot is pure redundancy — it's a constant per batch smuggled into every record.\n\nThat's actually the key insight for the verdict: with a fixed-layout wire, the record is deliberately context-free; discriminating information is *by design* metadata. So (b). And the raw reader stays honest because the wire contract makes no epoch claims at all — epoch semantics live one layer up. The reader's honesty = \"I can validate f64-ness and layout, nothing more; interpretation requires the source/frame envelope.\" If instead you put the discriminator in a slot (a), you've moved one bit of metadata into the record, which (i) burns a slot, (ii) creates a second truth that can contradict the source-level tag, (iii) tempts future sentinel semantics in numeric slots (the exact disease you just cured by rejecting the presence bit), and (iv) still doesn't give the raw reader the epoch *value* or its frame — so it's not actually self-describing, it's self-misdescribing.\n\nHmm, but wait. Let me reconsider: is there a scenario where records within one source/batch are mixed static and tv? No — ICGEM files are uniformly gfc or gfct per file. The discriminator is constant per dataset. Denormalizing a constant into every record is the classic anti-pattern.\n\nBut the \"strongest crack\" (stärkster Riss) — what is it? Let me think about what could break each option.\n\nOption (b) crack: the raw wire reader without source knowledge. If records can circulate detached from their source envelope (e.g., dumped to a file, logged, cached, forwarded), the discriminator is lost. A 26-f64 blob with no envelope is uninterpretable as to epoch semantics. If the pipeline ever slices/shards records away from their source header, (b) silently loses the truth. That's the strongest tear: **record/context separation** — the wire record is not self-contained, and any path that detaches records from their source envelope produces ambiguous data with no detection. Mitigation: contract must forbid naked records (every record travels in its source envelope), or add a checksum/batch-id.\n\nOption (a) crack: dual truth + slot cost + enum-in-f64 type abuse; per-record constant can disagree with source tag (drift), and 0.0/1.0 in a numeric slot invites sentinel misuse.\n\nOption (c) crack: two writers of the same fact = the fact can disagree; you must define which wins (source-level wins, record is a cached copy), and every reader must implement the precedence rule. Cost: complexity + validation burden. Benefit: raw reader gets *a* signal (though only 1 bit — it can refuse to interpret epoch for static, but it still doesn't know the epoch value or TDB frame... well the epoch is at source level anyway).\n\nWait — I should double check where epoch lives, because the question's phrasing matters for \"was enumerriert er genau\".\n\n\"epoch = TDB-Sekunden seit J2000, 0.0 = J2000.0 (echter Wert)\" — they define epoch semantics. \"Quellen tragen schon ein source-level Option-Epochenfeld (catalog_epoch, ref_epoch)\". So sources have optional epoch fields. The presence of these fields was presumably the old discriminator (presence-bit), being rejected. Good.\n\nSo the new discriminator: model_kind enum. Where? Source level (next to the epoch fields). What does it enumerate? I'd argue: static | time_variable (domain truth), with epoch-presence as a derived invariant, NOT epoch-applicable|not as the enum itself. Reasons:\n1. The domain concept is stable; \"epoch-applicable\" is a schema consequence. Enumerate the cause, derive the effect.\n2. ICGEM already speaks gfc/gfct — 1:1 mapping, no translation loss at ingestion.\n3. If you enumerate epoch-applicable|not, you can't distinguish \"static model\" from \"time-variable model whose epoch is unknown/missing\" — wait, actually with epoch-applicable|not: \"not applicable\" = static; \"applicable\" = tv. Same partition. But future kinds (e.g., trend models, periodic terms, multi-epoch series) would multiply applicability states without saying what the model *is*. The domain enum extends naturally (static | time_variable | trend | ...), and each kind carries its own epoch rules. Applicability-enum doesn't compose: a trend model has a reference epoch AND is \"time-dependent\" — the applicability axis is too poor.\n\nHmm, but there's a counter-argument for epoch-applicable|not: the question hints the consumer question is \"may I use epoch?\" and YAGNI says don't model physics you don't need. And \"static|zeitvariabel\" embeds ICGEM vocabulary into the contract, coupling to an external format's taxonomy. Hmm. But the house already ingests ICGEM and tags it; the contract is internal. I think the strongest answer: enumerate the model kind (static | time_variable), because that's the fact that exists in the world and in ICGEM; define epoch applicability as a contract invariant (static ⇒ epoch field absent; tv ⇒ epoch present, 0.0 valid). This gives you one truth and a validation rule instead of a second enum. And it directly kills the presence-bit ambiguity: presence is now *consequence*, not *signal*.\n\nNow \"Wie bleibt ein roher Wire-Leser ohne Quellwissen ehrlich?\"\n- The honest raw reader asserts only what the wire contract guarantees: 26 f64, fixed layout, layout identical for all model kinds. It must NOT interpret any slot as epoch or discriminator. It stays honest by refusing to attach epoch semantics: epoch is not on the wire (or if one slot IS the epoch... hmm).\n\nWait, I need to decide: is one of the 26 slots the epoch? The question: \"Wire = fix 26xf64, kein Umbau\" and they ask \"Wo muss der Unterscheider leben — (a) pro Record in einem 26xf64-Slot\". This implies slots are a possible home for the discriminator. And epoch: \"epoch = TDB-Sekunden seit J2000\" — is the epoch in a slot? If the epoch were in a slot, then for static models that slot would be... unused? sentinel? And the presence-bit rejection + \"0.0 = echter Wert\" strongly suggests the epoch occupies a slot and the old design used presence/zero-sentinel to mark static — which fails because 0.0 is a real epoch (J2000). Hmm, actually the presence-bit was probably on the source-level *optional* epoch field (catalog_epoch/ref_epoch sind \"Option-Epochenfeld\"). Optional field = presence bit. They reject presence-as-truth.\n\nHmm, so where does the epoch *value* live for a gfct record? Two possibilities:\n1. Per-record in one of the 26 slots (each record carries its epoch). Then for gfc records that slot is meaningless → need discriminator to know whether to read it. That's why they ask!\n2. Source-level (one epoch per dataset). Then records are pure coefficients.\n\nThe mention \"Quellen tragen schon ein source-level Option-Epochenfeld\" suggests epoch-at-source exists as precedent. But for a time-variable model, per-dataset epoch might be insufficient if a dataset contains multiple epochs (a time series). ICGEM gfct files are \nScroll to latest message\nMessage\nGLM 5.3\nSmall model\n·\nLinkedIn\n·\nYouTube\n·\nSubstack\n·\nPrivacy\n·\nTerms\n·\nAPI"